                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h2]Hauntingly beautiful and badass dark fantasy P&C adventure, BUT the moral tally of the ending is pure bs...[/h2]\n\n[b]Puzzle difficulty:[/b] ★★☆☆☆\n[u]Playtime:[/u] 4-5 hrs\n\nThe highlight of the game is the incredible artstyle and worldbuilding. There are very few games like this out there. You will meet interesting characters, wander crazy locations, operate and repair old, decaying machinery. The puzzles are decent, but relatively easy. I enjoyed the story too. \n\nBut you will have 10 moral choices along your journey that will decide which ending you get (the good or bad). If you want to get the 'good' ending, you will have to fully commit to it, and I mean FULLY. Don't do anything that could be even remotely considered 'bad', otherwise you will just default to the bad ending. I wanted to do a good playthrough first, and made 8 choices the game considered good and 2 the game decided is bad according to an arbitrary moral standard. One of the 2 'bad' choices was me killing a guard who literally was in the process of tormenting a poor guy. But the game decided that it is bad, so I got the bad ending and went to hell (???). Excuse me but thats total bs. I stand firm that was the good thing to do. So JFYK, there is almost no leeway here. \n\nAnyway, I still enjoyed this journey, but the \"weighting you heart against a feather\" ending kind of soured it for me.",
